<Base title={title} description={description} published={published} updated={updated}>
  <h1>Architecture marketing benchmark report 2026</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Someone has quoted you a cost per lead. Follow that number back to where it came from and
    the trail usually ends at dentists.
  </p>

  <p>
    The largest public Facebook advertising benchmark set publishes cost per lead for fourteen industries.
    Architecture is not one of them. Neither is construction. So when a firm gets told what a lead "should"
    cost, the number is almost always borrowed from a category that sells a $9,000 job to
    someone who decided this week, then quietly applied to a practice that sells a $2 million
    build to someone who has been thinking about it for three years.
  </p>

  <p>
    This report is our attempt to fix that for our own corner of the market. Every figure below
    comes out of ad accounts we run for US architecture and design firms, re-pulled from the
    reporting tables on 18 November 2026. Aggregate and anonymized, with the sample size attached to
    each claim, including the claims that make us look expensive.
  </p>

  <p>
    Third edition. July ran on sixteen accounts and ten months of spend. September ran on
    twenty-one accounts and a full twelve months, and overturned two of the July findings. This
    edition covers the twelve months to 17 November 2026 across twenty-six accounts, and it
    downgrades one more finding. What moved since September has its own section below, with the
    old figures left on the page so anything that quoted them can still be checked.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>Across 26 US architecture and design firm Meta ad accounts, $161,236 of spend and 3,427 leads between 18 November 2025 and 17 November 2026: the median firm paid $52 per lead, with the middle half landing between $30 and $69 and the full range running $13 to $88. About 9 in 100 people who clicked through filled in the form. Roughly 35 percent of leads turned into a booked consultation, at about $136 of ad spend each. A typical full month was $1,529 of spend and 24 leads.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why nobody has published this before</h2>
  <p>
    We went looking for prior art first, on the assumption that somebody must already benchmark
    paid social for architecture firms. Nobody does. LocaliQ and WordStream publish the <a href="https://localiq.com/blog/facebook-advertising-benchmarks/" rel="nofollow">most-cited Facebook benchmark study there is</a>, and we re-read its 2026 edition, published 23 September 2026, on 29 September. Fourteen cost per lead rows, from dentists to real estate. No architecture row, no construction row, no design row.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trade research that does exist for the profession, from SMPS and Deltek and the AIA,
    covers what firms spend as a share of revenue and how they win work. It does not publish
    what a lead costs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The nearest published anchor is Home and Home Improvement at $42.95 per lead, against an
    all-industry average of $27.39 for lead campaigns (LocaliQ and WordStream, Facebook
    Advertising Benchmarks, 2026 edition, last updated 23 September 2026). Those are the numbers a principal is
    unknowingly compared against. Neither was measured on anyone selling architectural services.
  </p>

  <h2>The benchmark table</h2>
  <p>
    Quartiles, not averages. One loud account can drag an average anywhere, so each row below
    is built from the accounts as separate results and then cut at the 25th, 50th and 75th
    percentile. Twenty-four of the 26 accounts produced at least ten leads in the window, and
    those 24 are the rows. If your own number sits at or under the median column, you are in the
    better half of the accounts we run.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric (per firm, n=24)</th><th>Better quarter</th><th>Median firm</th><th>Costlier quarter</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cost per lead</td><td>under $30</td><td>$52</td><td>over $69</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per link click</td><td>under $2.87</td><td>$3.94</td><td>over $5.57</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per click, all clicks</td><td>under $1.74</td><td>$2.62</td><td>over $2.99</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per 1,000 impressions</td><td>under $57</td><td>$73</td><td>over $99</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Link click-through rate</td><td>over 2.40%</td><td>1.89%</td><td>under 1.59%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clicks that became a lead</td><td>over 12.2%</td><td>9.2%</td><td>under 6.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Leads per $1,000 of spend</td><td>over 33</td><td>19.4</td><td>under 14.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Leads that booked a consultation</td><td>over 45%</td><td>31%</td><td>under 17%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per booked consultation</td><td>under $102</td><td>$177</td><td>over $277</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Two click rows, because the word "click" is doing something sneaky. Meta's headline cost per
    click counts every click on the ad, including reactions, comments and taps on the firm's
    profile photo. The click that can actually turn into a lead is the one that opens the ad's
    destination.
  </p>
  <p>
    In these accounts the median firm paid $2.62 for the first kind and $3.94 for the second, so
    the number worth planning against runs about half again higher than the number the dashboard
    shows you first.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full cost per lead range across the 24 accounts was $12.58 to $88.33. That is a factor
    of seven between the cheapest and the most expensive practice buying leads in the same
    country, in the same year, on the same platform.
  </p>
  <p>
    That spread is the actual finding. There is no single architecture cost per lead to hit, and
    any agency quoting you one number with a straight face is quoting you a median at best.
  </p>

  <h2>What moved since the September edition</h2>
  <p>
    Ten weeks, six new accounts, and the window slid forward to take in this autumn. One account
    left the sample on purpose: September counted an engineering firm, and this edition excludes it
    as a different profession. The September figures are on the left so that any page still quoting
    them, ours included, can be traced back to a number that was published here.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>September edition (21 accounts, to 11 Sep 2026)</th><th>November edition (26 accounts, to 17 Nov 2026)</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Ad spend in the window</td><td>$117,656</td><td>$161,236</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Leads</td><td>2,419</td><td>3,427</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Median cost per lead</td><td>$50</td><td>$52</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Middle half of cost per lead</td><td>$28 to $71</td><td>$30 to $69</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Median cost per 1,000 impressions</td><td>$64</td><td>$73</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Median cost per link click</td><td>$3.60</td><td>$3.94</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Median click-to-lead rate</td><td>9.1%</td><td>9.2%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Leads that booked, pooled</td><td>31%</td><td>35%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per booked consultation, pooled</td><td>$159</td><td>$136</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost per booked consultation, median firm</td><td>$148</td><td>$177</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Live days with no lead</td><td>41%</td><td>37%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Reaching people got more expensive. The median account now pays $73 per thousand
    impressions against $64 in September, about 14 percent more. The people who clicked turned
    into leads at the same rate as before, 9.2 percent against 9.1, so the ads themselves did not
    get worse. Most of the extra cost of a click came from the price of the audience.
  </p>
  <p>
    The median cost per lead barely moved through all of that, $50 to $52, and the middle half
    of the table actually narrowed. A lead is roughly the price it was in September even though
    the attention that produces it costs more.
  </p>
  <p>
    The booking rows pull in opposite directions, and both are true. Pooled across every lead,
    more of them booked and each booking cost less, $136 against $159, because several of the
    larger accounts that joined over the summer book a high share of their leads.
  </p>
  <p>
    The median firm went the other way, from $148 to $177. Six accounts launched after the
    September pull, five of them have enough leads to count, and their median is $198 per booked
    consultation against $126 for the rest.
    Part of that is timing: a booking trails its lead, and those accounts have had weeks rather
    than months for the bookings to land. We will know how much of it is timing at the next edition.
  </p>

  <h2>What a normal month looks like</h2>
  <p>
    Across 42 full calendar months of account activity, the median month was $1,529 of ad spend
    producing 24 leads. The quieter quarter of months came in at $1,170 and 19 leads. The busier
    quarter ran $3,053 and 49 leads. That is the honest shape of a small firm's advertising
    month, and it is a long way from the volume language used in most pitches.
  </p>
  <p>
    Twenty-four leads a month sounds thin until you price it against the work. One signed residential project at a typical 8 to 15 percent fee usually pays for a year of that spend several times over, which is the whole argument for <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects#how-much-should-you-spend">running a lead generation system at all</a> rather than waiting for the phone.
  </p>

  <h2>Leads arrive faster than firms expect, then stop for days</h2>
  <p>
    Two numbers that sit uncomfortably next to each other. Twenty-two accounts launched inside
    this window. Of the 21 that have produced a lead, the median got its first one a day after
    launch, 20 got one inside a week, and the slowest took 9 days. The 22nd went live four days
    before this pull and had not produced one yet. So the machine tends to prove itself quickly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then, across 2,253 account days of live spend, 37 percent produced no leads at all. Nearly
    every account we run has weeks with a two day silence in the middle of a perfectly healthy
    month. Firms who check the dashboard daily read that silence as failure and start turning
    things off, which is the single most expensive habit in this whole business.
  </p>
  <p>
    July put that figure at 47 percent and September at 41. It keeps falling as the sample grows,
    and it is still better than one day in three.
  </p>

  <h2>Findings we have taken back</h2>
  <p>
    A benchmark that never contradicts itself is a benchmark nobody is re-running. Three findings
    across two editions did not survive a larger sample, and the shape of each error is more
    useful than the original claim was.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Cost per lead does not reliably climb as an account ages.</strong> July reported a
    drift from about $41 in month one to about $71 in month four. September showed that was mostly
    an artifact of when accounts launched: most of the newest ones started in a cheap stretch of
    summer, which flattered month one and made everything after it look like decay.
  </p>
  <p>
    This edition indexes every account month against what the whole sample paid in that same
    calendar month. Months one to three and months four to six both sit at 1.14 times the sample
    (20 and 18 account months). Months seven and later sit at 1.46 (16 account months across five
    accounts), with the middle account month at 1.17.
  </p>
  <p>
    So there is a late-tenure signal, it now rests on five accounts instead of three, and it is
    modest once one or two expensive months stop dominating the average. Creative fatigue and a
    thinning local audience are real and worth getting ahead of. We are still not willing to tell
    you they arrive on a schedule.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Daily budget does not predict cost per lead.</strong> July found the accounts spending
    under $40 a day had the lowest cost per lead. September had them highest. This edition puts the
    $40 to $80 band highest at a $59 median, against $50 above $80 a day and $48 under $40 (two
    accounts). Three editions, three different orders. Spend level is not the lever, and we cannot
    find one in this data.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Spring is not reliably the expensive quarter.</strong> September said the same account
    pays about a quarter more per lead in April through June than in January through March. On
    this window the cheap half of that held up and the expensive half did not, which is the next
    section.
  </p>

  <h2>Winter is the cheap quarter. Spring is less clear than we said</h2>
  <p>
    Compare each account month against that same account's own average for the year, so a firm's
    local market and offer cancel out and only the timing is left. January through March runs at
    0.91 times an account's own year at the middle account month (10 account months, four
    accounts). All four accounts that advertised through the winter had their cheapest month of
    the window between December and March.
  </p>
  <p>
    April through June averages 1.47, which looks dramatic, and the middle account month is 1.08.
    Three account months carry almost all of that average: two from one account in its early
    months, and one bad May at another. Take them out and the spring average drops to 1.08, in
    line with the rest of the year.
    July through September sits at 0.92 and October through December at 1.13 (eight accounts each).
  </p>
  <p>
    Our read on winter, and it is a read rather than a proven mechanism: fewer businesses in the
    home and building trades advertise in the dead of winter, so an architecture firm is bidding
    against fewer remodelers and builders for the same attention. The people planning a building
    project are still online.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical version is unglamorous. Do not cut budget in January because it looks quiet when it is typically the cheapest stretch you will get, and do not judge a channel on one expensive month in any season. The wider seasonal pattern in buyer demand itself is mapped in <a href="/architecture-client-demand-by-metro-2026">architecture client demand by metro</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Lead forms against landing pages</h2>
  <p>
    Splitting campaigns by where the lead was actually captured, 36 campaigns across 20 accounts
    ran only on Facebook's own lead forms and produced 1,683 leads at $41.84 blended. Eleven campaigns
    across 8 accounts sent traffic only to a landing page and produced 617 leads at $67.96 blended.
    Another 8 campaigns ran both and came in at $41.60. Across the whole sample, 72 percent of leads
    arrived through an in-platform form.
  </p>
  <p>
    So in-platform forms typically come in around 40 percent cheaper per lead, the same gap
    September found. That is not an argument that landing pages are wrong, and eleven campaigns is
    still a small sample. A landing page also asks for more effort, which tends to hand you a better
    conversation with fewer people.
  </p>
  <p>
    Firms with strong <a href="/website-design-for-architects#what-a-site-that-converts-actually-has">web pages built to convert rather than to impress</a> often do better on the page route than this table suggests. The full method behind both routes is in <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>One campaign does most of the work</h2>
  <p>
    Restricting to the ten accounts that ran three or more campaigns, so the number is not just
    an artifact of accounts that only ever ran one, the single best campaign produced 80 percent
    of that account's leads at the median. The weakest concentration in that group was 37
    percent from one campaign, and the strongest was 98 percent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Across the whole sample, 23 percent of spend went to campaigns that were not the account's
    best one, and 8 of 63 campaigns produced no leads at all on $2,009 between them. Most of that
    spend bought the search rather than the result. You cannot know which campaign is the one
    until several have run.
  </p>
  <p>
    That ratio is the practical case for volume in production and simplicity in execution. You are not looking for a set of good campaigns, you are looking for the one that works, and the way to find it is to put up several and let the losers die quickly. It is also why <a href="/video-marketing-for-architects#what-to-actually-film">a steady supply of raw footage and project photography</a> matters more than any single polished asset.
  </p>
  <p>
    Method note for anyone comparing editions: July reported this at the individual ad level and
    found 56 percent of ads produced zero leads. September and this edition report campaigns, and we
    have not restated the July figure as though the two were interchangeable.
  </p>

  <h2>From lead to booked consultation</h2>
  <p>
    Of 3,427 leads, 1,187 became a booked consultation inside the client's own advertising window.
    That is 35 percent pooled, at about $136 of ad spend per booked consultation. Per account,
    the median was 31 percent, the better quarter cleared 45 percent and the weaker quarter came
    in under 17 percent.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read that spread carefully, because part of it is measurement rather than performance. Some
    firms book consultations in systems we can see and some book them over the phone into a
    calendar we cannot, so the low end of that range mixes slow follow-up with incomplete
    tracking. When those bookings happen, by hour, weekday and how far ahead, is broken out in <a href="/consultation-booking-for-architects">consultation booking for architects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    We are still not publishing a cost per consultation that actually showed up, and we checked
    again for this edition. Of the 1,187 bookings, 97 were later canceled and only 6 carry a
    recorded outcome of held or no-show. A show rate built on that would be a number about our
    record-keeping. When it is solid it goes on this page.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the spread does establish is where the money leaks. A firm at 17 percent and a firm at 45 percent are usually buying the same leads at the same price, and the difference is speed of response and who picks up the phone, which is the part of <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects#content-and-follow-up">the follow-up system</a> nobody wants to own.
  </p>

  <h2>Residential and commercial read differently</h2>
  <p>
    Ten of the 26 accounts carry a segment on their record, eight residential and two
    commercial. September had three commercial accounts, and one of them was the engineering firm
    this edition excludes, so this section is a direction and nothing firmer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The residential accounts paid $41.51 per lead and booked 38.3 percent of those leads, landing
    at $108.47 per booked consultation. The commercial accounts paid $46.25 per lead, about 11
    percent more, then booked 51.9 percent of those leads and landed at $89.09 per booked
    consultation.
  </p>
  <p>
    The direction matches September's. A commercial lead costs more, and a larger share of
    commercial leads turn into a meeting, so the two segments converge or cross at the metric that
    matters. A firm reading only the cost per lead column will conclude commercial work is expensive
    to market, and the booking column says otherwise.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you design homes, expect the shape in this report: a cheap first week, a $30 to $69 cost per lead band, a lot of unqualified curiosity mixed in, and homeowners who need several conversations before they are a project. The detail work for that segment sits in <a href="/marketing-for-residential-architects">marketing for residential architects</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you design for developers, owners and businesses, expect a higher cost per lead, a longer evaluation, and a better show of intent per inquiry. Both commercial accounts here ran a specific offer aimed at someone comparing firms rather than a soft form, which fits what we see in <a href="/marketing-for-commercial-architects">marketing for commercial architects</a>: the same engine aimed at a buyer who is evaluating rather than dreaming.
  </p>

  <h2>How these numbers compare to the published ones</h2>
  <p>
    Our median firm pays $2.62 a click on the same all-clicks basis the published studies use,
    where the published all-industry Facebook figure for lead campaigns is $1.80. Our median account converts 9.2
    percent of link clicks into leads where the published click-to-lead figure is 8.54 percent.
    So the ads themselves perform normally.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our median cost per lead of $52 sits well above the
    published all-industry $27.39 and above the $42.95 for Home and Home Improvement.
  </p>
  <p>
    The published study's 2026 edition barely moved from the one before it: the all-industry
    figure slipped from $27.66 to $27.39, while Home and Home Improvement edged up from $41.26 to
    $42.95. Fresher benchmarks do not close the gap.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most of it is the market. An architecture firm advertises to one or two counties, not a
    nation, and the pool of people about to commission a custom building in your county this
    quarter is small. And some of it is simply what a serious lead costs when the thing being
    sold takes a year to sign.
  </p>
  <p>
    For scale on the other side of the ledger, the same body of research puts the average Google search cost per lead at $70.11, and a click from someone typing "architect near me" into Google runs around $10 before anyone fills in anything. Our read on that trade sits in <a href="/google-ads-for-architects#what-google-ads-cost-for-architects">Google Ads for architects</a>, and the wider channel picture is mapped in <a href="/architecture-marketing">the architecture marketing guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>

  <h3>How many leads should an architecture firm expect from Facebook ads?</h3>
  <p>
    In our accounts the median full month produced 24 leads on $1,529 of spend, with the middle
    band running 19 to 49 leads. Expressed per dollar, the median account got 19.4 leads per
    $1,000 and the best quarter got over 33.
  </p>
  <p>
    If someone promises you 100 leads a month at a small firm's budget, ask what they count as a
    lead and what share of them booked anything.
  </p>

  <h3>How long does it take for architecture ads to start producing leads?</h3>
  <p>
    Faster than most principals expect. The median account launched in this window saw its first
    lead one day after launch and 20 of 21 saw one within a week, with the slowest at 9 days.
  </p>
  <p>
    The harder wait is the one after that. Judging a campaign inside its first two weeks of
    volume is how firms talk themselves out of a channel, and 37 percent of live account days
    produced no leads at all, so a quiet stretch means very little on its own.
  </p>

  <h3>Why did my cost per lead go up after a few months?</h3>
  <p>
    Check the calendar and the market before you blame the account. Compared against what the
    whole sample paid in the same calendar month, months one to six are flat in our data, and the
    later months show a modest rise on five accounts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Across the whole sample, the price of reaching people rose about 14 percent between September
    and November while click-to-lead held steady, so some of what feels like an account getting
    worse is the platform getting dearer. Creative fatigue and a thinning local audience still bite,
    and new creative and a wider radius are still the fix.
  </p>
  <p>
    Ad by ad the picture is the same: our longest-running winners lost only about 12 percent of their efficiency after week eight, measured in <a href="/facebook-ad-fatigue-for-architects">how often architects should change their Facebook ads</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Is there a cheap time of year to advertise an architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    Winter, typically. January through March came in at 0.91 times an account's own yearly cost
    per lead, and all four accounts that advertised through the winter had their cheapest month
    between December and March.
  </p>
  <p>
    We used to say spring was the expensive quarter. On a larger sample that rested on three bad
    account months, so we no longer say it. At the middle account month, every quarter sits within
    about 15 percent of an account's own average.
  </p>

  <h3>Are Facebook lead forms better than a landing page for architects?</h3>
  <p>
    Cheaper per lead, typically. Lead form campaigns in this sample came in at $41.84 per lead
    against $67.96 for landing page campaigns, and 72 percent of all leads arrived through a
    form.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whether cheaper is better depends on what happens next, since a form that takes eight seconds
    to complete sends you people who invested eight seconds. Most of our accounts run forms as
    the volume engine and treat the landing page as the higher-intent route.
  </p>

  <h3>What does a booked consultation cost an architecture firm?</h3>
  <p>
    About $136 of ad spend at the pooled rate, and $177 for the median account, with the middle
    half of accounts between $102 and $277. Residential accounts landed at $108.47 and commercial
    accounts at $89.09.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the number worth holding. A lead is an intermediate metric and a consultation in the
    diary is the thing you can actually sell into. Price it against what one signed project pays
    you and the channel usually stops looking expensive.
  </p>

  <h2>How this report was built</h2>
  <p>
    Sample: 26 US architecture and design firm Meta ad accounts managed by IPQmedia, all spend
    between 18 November 2025 and 17 November 2026, re-pulled from our reporting tables on 18
    November 2026. Totals: $161,236 of ad spend, 2,460,210 impressions, 43,829 link clicks,
    3,427 leads, 1,187 booked consultations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two construction contractor accounts and one
    engineering firm in the same reporting tables were excluded, because they are a different
    profession and a different buyer. The engineering firm was inside the September edition's 21
    accounts, so the two editions differ by that account as well as by the window.
  </p>
  <p>
    Method notes. Per-firm figures treat each account as one observation and report quartiles, so
    a single high-volume account cannot move the median. The quartile table uses the 24 accounts
    with at least ten leads in the window. Pooled figures divide total spend by total results and
    are labeled as such wherever they appear. Booked consultations are counted only inside each
    account's own advertising window, including the ones later canceled.
  </p>
  <p>
    Click-based rows exclude 9 account days where the export reported more link clicks than total
    clicks, which is a reporting artifact rather than a result. A correction while we are here: the
    September edition's total of 81,038 link clicks included its 25 artifact days, most of them in
    one account's first weeks in September 2025. Its click rows excluded them and were right. The
    total was inflated, and this window no longer contains those days.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tenure analysis normalizes each account month against the whole sample's cost per lead for
    that calendar month, which is how we separated account age from seasonality. The seasonal
    analysis does the reverse, normalizing each account month against that account's own annual
    cost per lead, which is how we separated season from account mix. Both restrict to account
    months with at least 20 days of spend and at least 5 leads, 54 in this window.
  </p>
  <p>
    Limits, stated up front because a benchmark without them is marketing. Twenty-six accounts is
    a small sample and the commercial segment rests on two of them. Only four accounts advertised
    through the winter and only one ran in every month of the window, which is why the seasonal
    section names medians and counts rather than one clean curve.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rules we apply to every figure on this site, our own and everyone else's, are written down in <a href="/editorial-standards">our editorial standards</a>,
    along with a dated log of the corrections we have had to make.
  </p>
  <p>
